# bracket-operations-with-explicit-pre-and-post-checks

Every non-trivial operation should be surrounded by two explicit verification gates: (1) a pre-condition gate that confirms all prerequisites actually exist in the current system state before the operation begins, and (2) a post-condition gate that enumerates each intended target or outcome and confirms it was actually achieved. This bracket pattern recurred in three distinct forms across the session: verifying a root cause still holds before applying a fix (pre), ensuring prerequisite data/components are present before running a task that depends on them (pre), and enumerating intended targets after execution and checking each one was processed (post). Neither gate is optional.

## Procedures
- Before acting: list all preconditions the operation depends on (data present, root cause confirmed, config valid) and verify each holds in the current state.
- After acting: list all intended targets or outcomes and verify each was actually achieved — do not rely on exit codes or absence of exceptions.
- Treat any unverified precondition or unconfirmed outcome as a latent defect, not a minor gap.

